result:
Fixed. The cause was in the abstract-socket branches of `UnixListener::bind` and `UnixStream::connect`:

- `tokio/src/net/unix/listener.rs:84`
- `tokio/src/net/unix/stream.rs:80`

Both detected a leading NUL to identify an abstract path, then passed the **whole** byte slice — NUL included — to `std::os::unix::net::SocketAddr::from_abstract_name`. That constructor prepends the NUL itself, so `"\0aaa"` was registered as abstract name `"\0aaa"`, i.e. sockaddr bytes `"\0\0aaa"`. Bind and connect still agreed with each other (both were wrong identically), which is why the connection succeeded and the bug stayed silent — but `local_addr().as_abstract_name()` returned `"\0aaa"` instead of `"aaa"`, and any peer using the name correctly would fail to reach the socket.

The fix drops the leading NUL before handing the name over:

```rust
if os_str_bytes.starts_with(b"\0") {
    // `from_abstract_name` prepends the leading null byte itself,
    // so it must not be included in the name we pass in.
    StdSocketAddr::from_abstract_name(&os_str_bytes[1..])?
} else {
    StdSocketAddr::from_pathname(path)?
}
```

Verification: `abstract_socket_name` fails on the pre-fix tree (`assertion left == right failed` at `uds_stream.rs:444`) and passes after; the full `uds_stream` suite (8 tests) plus `uds_datagram`, `uds_socket`, `uds_split`, and `uds_cred` all pass.
